ture half: candidate WIs or open items filed through intake, and the critic never edits the spine. A hat that finds a missing obligation yields an SR candidate under that need, naming the hat for its `hat_refs`.\r\n\r\n**One action or two** (ruled in revision 35: one `run.cmd audit` with `arch` and `hats` parts, as recommended below). A hat recheck is not architecture, so `arch-audit` misnames an action that holds both. Recommended: one manual audit, `run.cmd audit`, whose parts (`arch`, `hats`) run together by default or alone by name, sharing the findings bundle and the intake route. The alternative is a sibling `run.cmd hats-audit` of the same shape. Either way the action ships in the run-menu template, inside item 12's one RESYNC entry.\r\n\r\n### First step: a dry run on W3, before building anything\r\n\r\nGive an independent model only the drafted rubric and W3 Autonomy's current shape: its modules, internal seams, the lane's runtime flow (Flow 4) and its interfaces. Ask it for candidate decompositions, compared on the decisions each hides, cohesion and dependency reduction.\r\n- **The rubric works if its proposal is argued from evidence**: which decisions each cut hides, which dependencies it removes, and what it costs, such as IF-175's shared verdict service. The proposal may reject a stage split. Disagreeing with the lane stages a stakeholder would name is not a failure (revision 16, from Astra; Parnas 1972: decomposing by processing steps is the weaker criterion).\r\n- **If its proposal is unargued, or ignores a shared definition,** the rubric is fixed while it is still text.\r\n\r\nThe dry run also gives F-call 5 (recursive components) its first worked case.\r\n\r\n**Cost.**\r\n- **Text only (phase 1):** the rubric, the skill, the spine-questions pointer and the plan-decomposition question, about one lane.\r\n- **Builds (phase 3, after item 11's ruling):** checks A and C, the audit action and its critique skill.\r\n\r\n**Downstream.** The rubric, skill, pointer and plan-critique question ship to adopters as kit-owned text; the audit action ships in the run-menu template. It is one RESYNC entry; adopters migrate nothing, since everything is additive.\r\n\r\n**Decided by the owner (revision 15):** a skill, not a hat; the planner decides on an architectural delta, with no automatic trigger (a WI beyond its scope is handed back); the audit runs on demand. **Superseded in part by decision 4** (revision 18): the scope critique asks the delta question before the claim; the in-lane plan, and every re-plan, asks it again for within-scope design, and a beyond-scope shift found in the lane closes partial.\r\n\r\n**When the critique runs during the initial breakdown** (owner's position, revision 15; gates named in revision 16). Not at every component scope. `DevStg-Arch` has no approval act; the signed boundaries are DevStg-Reqs, DevStg-Tests and DevStg-Impl. So each critique gates an act that exists. Three moments:\r\n- **The top level.** Read as the first architecture step below the SRs: depth 0, where SRs are allocated to components and the first LLRs attach. The SRs themselves are already judged by their own sitting. **Gate:** the first LLR sitting at depth 0 refuses without that scope's critique verdict on file (the `critique-provenance` rubric exists).\r\n- **Once over the whole tree,** when the lower levels appear complete. **Gate:** entry to DevStg-Tests, not \"the build\".\r\n- **Every second level of component depth** away from the SRs, in a deep tree. A wrong split is then caught at most two levels after it is made, instead of only at the whole-tree pass. **Gate:** the first LLR sitting at that depth, as at depth 0. Component and IF rows between critiques stay Drafted. The cadence schedules the critique; it never approves anything unreviewed.\r\n\r\n**Ruled in revision 35:** \"top level\" is depth 0 as read above, and the every-two-levels rule applies always. The \"floors\" here are component depths, not spine tiers: the tiers stay SN, SR, LLR and TC, and the recursion is in the component tree and, since revision 35, in the LLRs' parent links (F-call 5).\r\n\r\n**How a planner picks up the skill** (owner's concern, revision 15). The architectural-delta question arises inside a WI, so the planner must meet the skill without being told each time. Today nothing routes skills to a planner:\r\n- **The frontmatter isn't read.** A skill's `phases` frontmatter (`dev`, `gate`, `release`) only feeds `skills/INDEX.csv`; no brief reads it.\r\n- **The planner prompt doesn't mention skills.** `plan_briefs.py` and `prompts/dual-plan-planner.template.md` name none.\r\n- **Model-dependent discovery.** A Claude session may find a skill from its `description` (\"Use when …\"); a Codex planner won't.\r\n\r\nSo the reliable route is the prompt. The planner template, and WI-805's single-plan prompt when it exists, carry one line naming the skill **by its shipped path**: answer the architectural-delta question by following `skills/architecture-decomposition/SKILL.md`. `plan_briefs.assemble` fills the template body into the planner's session, so a Codex planner reads it; that line is template text and needs a RESYNC entry (revision 16, both reviewers). The skill's `description` is written to trigger on planning, as a second route.\r\n\r\n**An architecture role** (owner's position, revision 15). PROCESS.md §1's role table gains an **Architect** row owning `components.toml` (CMP) and `interfaces.toml` (IF), the rows no role owns today. The planner wears it whenever the architectural-delta question comes up, and the author wears it at `DevStg-Arch`. Whether `runtime-flows.md` moves from the Software Engineer to the Architect was open, **ruled in revision 35:** the Architect owns it. Its state flows are the Architect's mode classes; its sequence detail is the engineer's.\r\n\r\n**Code-hygiene instruments in the audit** (owner, revision 15, from a separate session's tool list). The audit's mechanical half covers the code level beside the architecture level. In-kit, stdlib-only:\r\n- duplication: `check_dupes_census`;\r\n- complexity and size: `check_complexity` (cognitive complexity and SLOC);\r\n- `check_readability`.\r\n\r\nOptional, declared by the adopter for their own stack (CLAUDE.md: tools a downstream project needs are theirs to install, and the kit must not require them to run its own checks):\r\n- duplication across languages: jscpd;\r\n- dead code: knip for JS/TS (unused files, exports and dependencies), vulture for Python;\r\n- complexity: lizard (any language) or radon (Python);\r\n- shell: shellcheck and PSScriptAnalyzer.\r\n\r\nThe audit runs each declared tool as a step and reads its output; it never changes code. **The kit has no dead-code check at all.** Whether a stdlib one belongs in the kit (unused module-level functions found by `ast`, with the false positives of dynamic dispatch) is a separate call, not part of this item. Python examples are marked \"swap for your stack\".\r\n\r\n**Found on the way:** PROCESS.md §1's role table names no owner for the CMP and IF rows. The Architect role proposed above answers it.\r\n\r\n196:[need.SN-012]\n197-status = \"Approved\"\n198-stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-02\"]\n199-tags = [\"process\"]\n200-need =\"\"\"A team can keep small changes small: the process is **right-sized**, not ceremony for its own sake — small changes stay cheap, and heavy layers are opt-in.\"\"\"\n201-why = \"An over-heavy process is abandoned; adoption depends on proportionality.\"\n202-priority = \"S\"\n203-acceptance = \"\"\"Opt-in layers (perf, guardrails, unattended, parallel tracks, OKF export) cost a repo that doesn't use them nothing; the proportionality doctrine governs LLR/TC granularity.\"\"\"\n204-\n205-[need.SN-023]\n206-status = \"Approved\"\n207-stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-03\"]\n208-# Both tags are INERT AS ROUTING and kept as SUBJECT metadata. `ui`: the UX\n209-# pair is `always` here and gated on `render`/`ui` in the SHIPPED template (the\n--\n336:[need.SN-040]\n337-status = \"Approved\"\n338-stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-03\"]\n339-priority = \"S\"\n340-need = \"\"\"**Scope: template (adopters + this repo).** A stakeholder can obtain a repeatable explanation for why the system was divided into its chosen components, based on the system's declared inputs, outputs and internal signal trade-offs rather than an unrecorded model judgment.\"\"\"\n341-why = \"\"\"A component breakdown invented free-form by a model (or a person) leaves no record of the alternatives weighed, so the next reviser cannot tell load-bearing boundaries from accidents. Deriving the partition from the system's own declared inputs and outputs, against a declared objective, leaves evidence the choice can be re-examined against.\"\"\"\n342-acceptance = \"\"\"A decomposition run records the candidate partitions considered, the declared objective and constraints they were scored against, the selected partition, and a comparison a reviewer can reproduce; the human names the components and rules the final choice; the record is kept with the architecture, not in session prose.\"\"\"\n343-\n344-[need.SN-041]\n345-status = \"Approved\"\n346-stakeholder_refs = [\"STK-01\"]\n347-source = [\"README.md#objective-o1\"]\n348-priority = \"M\"\n349-need = \"\"\"**Scope: template (adopters + this repo).** A person reading a project's code or analytics long after it was written can understand what each part does and why it exists, and can change one part without having to understand or rework unrelated parts.\"\"\"\nrg: docs/interfaces.toml: The system cannot find the file specified. (os error 2)\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml:423:[interface.IF-019]\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-424-owner = \"scripts/gen_skills_index\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-425-consumers = [\"external:downstream adopter\"]\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-426-channel = \"file\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-427-data = \"skills/INDEX.csv: banner comment, header, one row per skill (name, scope, stacks, domains, phases, tags, description)\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-428-version = \"v1\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-429-status = \"Drafted\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-430-interface_to_external = \"B-05\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-431-coincident = \"\"\"The index row per skill is what an agent or a person reads to find a skill; the listing is the outcome.\"\"\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-432-\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-433-[interface.IF-020]\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-434-owner = \"scripts/subagent_gate\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-435-consumers = [\"external:agent CLI\"]\n--\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml:679:[interface.IF-035]\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-680-owner = \"project-trajectory/skills/\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-681-consumers = [\"scripts/gen_skills_index\", \"scripts/bootstrap\"]\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-682-channel = \"file\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-683-data = \"SKILL.md frontmatter: name, scope, stacks, domains, phases, tags; each other file in the skill directory, materialized with it at the same relative path\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-684-version = \"v1\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-685-status = \"Drafted\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-686-notes = \"\"\"Low fan-out: two direct walkers of a skill directory - bootstrap (select_skills filtering on the SKILL.md frontmatter at scaffold time, then skill_copies materializing and inventorying the whole directory) and gen_skills_index (the INDEX.csv generator, and the fan-out drift check comparing the whole file set). A closed, nameable set.\"\"\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-687-\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-688-[interface.IF-036]\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-689-owner = \"external:upstream docs\"\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml-690-consumers = [\"scripts/check_vendored\"]\ndocs/requirements/interfaces.toml