ajectory/PROCESS.md-941-records the gate decision there, cites it from status.md's *Stage* line,\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-942-and requests acceptance per the declared gate authority (§4).\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-943-\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-944-**Voice policy — warmth has a layer boundary.** Personality is a human value, not\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-945-a machine one: **human-facing** output (CLI narration, a kickoff greeting, a\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-946-release-checklist intro) may carry warmth and **dry wit at most**;\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-947-**machine/agent-facing** output — this protocol's findings and verdicts,\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-948-subagent prompts, registry cells, commit messages — stays **literal, terse,\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-949-structured: no whimsy**. Levity there costs tokens, reads ambiguously to the\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-950-next agent (irony/understatement is exactly what a parser misreads), and\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-951-erodes the honesty/severity signal this protocol depends on. Default voice is\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-952-**restrained**\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-953-(\"direct and concrete; dry wit at most; never at the expense of clarity or\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-954-honesty\"); a project may expose an optional, named **tone knob** to dial levity\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-955-up or down — never a baked-in persona, since no single tone fits both a\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-956-medical-device repo and a game studio.\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-957-\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-958-### Change intake — routing a problem to the spine\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-959-\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-960-An inbound problem routes by **which registry row it contradicts** — that\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-961-classification, not the fix, is step 1. Then check the parent obligation and\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-962-selected design before treating the violation as a coverage gap:\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-963-\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-964-```mermaid\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-965-flowchart TD\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-966-  P[\"problem identified\"] --> C{\"which row does it contradict?\"}\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-967-  C -->|\"an existing SR/LLR is violated\"| D{\"obligation and design still sound?\"}\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-968-  D -->|\"yes\"| TG[\"coverage gap:\\nno TC caught it\"]\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-969-  D -->|\"parent sound; LLR unsuitable\"| DR[\"amend LLR + verification\\npreserve parent acceptance\"]\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-970-  D -->|\"promised behavior changes\"| RG\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-971-  C -->|\"no row speaks to it\"| RG[\"requirement gap:\\nnew/changed SN -> SR -> LLR\\n(walk DevStg-Reqs/DevStg-Tests for that slice)\"]\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-972-  TG --> T1[\"write the failing TC first\"]\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-973-  RG --> S{\"scope the solution\"}\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-974-  S -->|\"new seam\"| I2[\"IF-### row\\n+ a TC per contract\"]\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-975-  S -->|\"new subsystem/part\"| C2[\"CMP-### / PART-### rows\\n+ Component tags\"]\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-976-  S -->|\"code only\"| L2[\"LLR rows on the\\nexisting modules\"]\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-977-  I2 --> W[\"WI-### into the DAG\"]\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-978-  C2 --> W\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-979-  L2 --> W\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-980-  T1 --> W\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-981-  DR --> W\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-982-  W --> G[\"implement test-first; gates re-run\"]\n[requirement.SR-165]\r\ntitle = \"The component partition carries a reproducible derivation record\"\r\nsn_refs = [\"SN-040\"]\r\nboundary_refs = [\"B-05\"]\r\nhat_refs = [\"MAINTAINER\"]\r\nrequirement = \"The delivered planning content shall leave, with every component partition it produces, a machine-readable derivation record in the tracked component registry rather than session prose — the candidate partitions considered, the declared objective and constraints they were scored against, each candidate's score, the selected partition, and the human ruling that named the components and adopted it — such that a live component row whose record omits any of those elements, or a selection the recorded scores do not rank first without a recorded human override, is a reportable finding.\"\r\nrationale = \"Realizes SN-040 — a free-form partition leaves no record of the alternatives weighed, so the next reviser cannot tell load-bearing boundaries from accidents; the record is what lets the choice be re-examined. SN-040 had no citing SR, even as the P5 partition work performed exactly this record. Reproducibility is stated as a recomputation from the record's own declared inputs, not as a reviewer re-running a model session, because only the former can fail — the SR-161 form applied to the partition instead of the perspective set. B-05 alone: the human ruling is an element the record must carry, not an observable the system states at the authority input, which is SR-139's and SR-140's ground.\"\r\nacceptance_criteria = \"A partition round writes the derivation record into the tracked component registry, never into session prose or a plan document alone; a live component row with no record, or a record omitting the candidate partitions, the declared objective, the constraints, any candidate's score, the selected partition or the human ruling, is reported naming the missing element; a selected partition the recorded scores do not rank first is reported unless the record carries the human override that chose it; the recorded ranking recomputes from the record's own objective, constraints and scores alone.\"\r\npriority = \"S\"\r\nverification = \"Test\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\nphase = 5\r\ncoincident = \"\"\"The need asks for a repeatable explanation of the component partition; the recorded candidates, objective, scores, selection and human ruling are that explanation, and a record missing any of them is reported.\"\"\"\r\naspect = \"process\"\r\n[requirement.SR-186]\r\ntitle = \"Proportionate requirement decomposition\"\r\nsn_refs = [\"SN-012\"]\r\nboundary_refs = [\"B-05\"]\r\nhat_refs = [\"MAINTAINER\", \"PRODUCT-FITNESS\"]\r\nrequirement = \"The delivered requirements process shall require each additional child within a required tier to carry an independent decision or verification purpose, and record the stopping decision in the scoped decomposition record, while retaining the required SN-to-SR-to-LLR-to-TC tiers and linking real verification to the existing obligation.\"\r\nrationale = \"Carries SN-012's unowned granularity clause as a process requirement on what the delivered process requires and records. A child that only paraphrases a parent or duplicates another child's verification adds review and maintenance cost without an independent decision. The process records the stopping decision within the required spine; this row does not guarantee adopter behavior beyond that record, and it never permits omitting a required SN→SR→LLR→TC tier or replacing real verification with a count. It does not impose a row-count cap, deletion quota, or new machine gate. Existing process doctrine and the spine-authoring adjudicator questions are the source of the judgment, while mechanism-specific evidence remains with its current SR.\"\r\nacceptance_criteria = \"For a reviewed decomposition, each additional child within a required tier has an independent decision or verification purpose traceable to its parent; when further splitting would only paraphrase or duplicate, the existing scoped decomposition/review record states why the process stops at that independent-value boundary.\"\r\npriority = \"S\"\r\nverification = \"Inspection\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\nphase = 5\r\ncoincident = \"\"\"The need asks for a decomposition that stays proportionate; each additional child's recorded purpose, and the recorded stopping decision, is that outcome, judged by review.\"\"\"\r\naspect = \"process\"\r\n[need.SN-012]\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\nstakeholder_refs = [\"STK-02\"]\r\ntags = [\"process\"]\r\nneed =\"\"\"A team can keep small changes small: the process is **right-sized**, not ceremony for its own sake — small changes stay cheap, and heavy layers are opt-in.\"\"\"\r\nwhy = \"An over-heavy process is abandoned; adoption depends on proportionality.\"\r\npriority = \"S\"\r\nacceptance = \"\"\"Opt-in layers (perf, guardrails, unattended, parallel tracks, OKF export) cost a repo that doesn't use them nothing; the proportionality doctrine governs LLR/TC granularity.\"\"\"\r\n[need.SN-040]\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\nstakeholder_refs = [\"STK-03\"]\r\npriority = \"S\"\r\nneed = \"\"\"**Scope: template (adopters + this repo).** A stakeholder can obtain a repeatable explanation for why the system was divided into its chosen components, based on the system's declared inputs, outputs and internal signal trade-offs rather than an unrecorded model judgment.\"\"\"\r\nwhy = \"\"\"A component breakdown invented free-form by a model (or a person) leaves no record of the alternatives weighed, so the next reviser cannot tell load-bearing boundaries from accidents. Deriving the partition from the system's own declared inputs and outputs, against a declared objective, leaves evidence the choice can be re-examined against.\"\"\"\r\nacceptance = \"\"\"A decomposition run records the candidate partitions considered, the declared objective and constraints they were scored against, the selected partition, and a comparison a reviewer can reproduce; the human names the components and rules the final choice; the record is kept with the architecture, not in session prose.\"\"\"\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
